---
const { current } = Astro.props as { current: 'cps' | 'keyboard' | 'ghosting' | 'spacebar' | 'reaction' };
---
{current === 'cps' && <section class="seo-content">
  <h2>How to get a useful CPS test result</h2>
  <p>A CPS test measures clicks per second: the number of clicks the page receives divided by the test duration. This test uses a fixed five-second round, so 25 recorded clicks produces 5.0 CPS and 40 clicks produces 8.0 CPS. A fixed length makes separate attempts easier to compare. Start with a relaxed hand position, place the pointer over the target, and use the same mouse or trackpad for each round. The first click starts the timer and is included in the score, so you do not need a separate start button or a practice click.</p>
  <h2>Use the CPS test with a repeatable method</h2>
  <p>Make three rounds rather than judging a single attempt. Rest for a few seconds between them, then compare the middle result with the highest result. The middle often gives a more representative snapshot because an unusually low round can come from a missed click, while an unusually high round can come from a short burst that is difficult to repeat. Keep the same clicking method for the comparison: a trackpad tap, a left mouse button, and alternate-finger clicking are different actions and should not be treated as interchangeable scores.</p>
  <h2>What changes a clicks-per-second score?</h2>
  <p>Click speed is affected by hand position, button resistance, travel distance, fatigue, the pointing device, and the browser’s ability to receive the input. A heavier office mouse can feel different from a lightweight gaming mouse even when both work correctly. Trackpads may register taps differently from physical switches. A score also changes when you switch from a steady single-finger press to a deliberate rapid technique. That is why this CPS test is best used as a personal, like-for-like comparison rather than as a universal ranking of devices or players.</p>
  <h2>Read CPS test metrics without overclaiming</h2>
  <p>The live Current CPS value is a running estimate while the five seconds are still passing. The Result CPS value after the timer ends is the one to write down, because it uses the complete fixed interval. A score of 6.4 means the browser received 32 clicks in five seconds; it does not prove that the mouse is electrically polling at a particular rate or that the same speed will be possible in every game. Browser event handling, background load, and a temporary loss of focus can all change what the page receives.</p>
  <h2>When a CPS test exposes a click problem</h2>
  <p>A low score by itself is not a fault. Look for a more specific pattern: does the target fail to react to normal presses, do clicks arrive in pairs when you are pressing once, or does the result change after the mouse is charged or reconnected? If you suspect accidental repeat input, use the dedicated <a href="/double-click-test">Double Click Test</a> and make 20 to 30 slow clicks. If other controls also seem unreliable, the <a href="/mouse-test">Mouse Test</a> checks buttons, movement, and scroll directions in one place.</p>
  <h2>Browser limits and click-event standards</h2>
  <p>This page counts click events delivered to the tab; it does not open the mouse, inspect switch wear, or read device firmware. The browser interaction model behind mouse and click events is defined by the <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/uievents/" rel="noopener noreferrer">W3C UI Events specification</a>. That distinction matters when comparing a browser score with results from a game or a manufacturer utility. Treat repeated, controlled runs as useful evidence, then test another browser or computer before concluding that a device needs repair or replacement.</p>
  <h2>Practical CPS test checklist</h2>
  <p>Use a stable surface, close anything that might steal focus, and avoid changing your technique during the round. Record the completed score from three runs, noting whether you used a mouse or trackpad. If your goal is practice, stop before your hand becomes uncomfortable; an aggressive technique can create strain and does not make a normal-use comparison more useful. If your goal is diagnostics, click calmly instead of rapidly, because a controlled sample makes missed or doubled input easier to separate from your own timing.</p>
</section>}

{current === 'keyboard' && <section class="seo-content">
  <h2>How to use this keyboard test</h2>
  <p>This keyboard test checks whether a key press reaches the focused browser panel. Click the detection area once, then press each key you want to verify. The page shows the visible key value, the browser key code, and the number of distinct keys it has detected. Start with letters, numbers, arrows, and modifier keys one at a time. Testing slowly matters: it helps distinguish a key that never arrives from a key that was pressed while another app, browser shortcut, or system feature had focus.</p>
  <h2>What the keyboard test records</h2>
  <p>Browsers expose a key value and a physical-style code for keyboard interaction. For example, the visible value can reflect a layout while the code helps identify the physical key location. This keyboard test shows both so that a key such as Shift, Enter, ArrowLeft, or Space is easier to describe accurately. The <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/uievents-key/" rel="noopener noreferrer">W3C KeyboardEvent key values specification</a> documents the values browsers use for these events. A detected event is evidence that this tab received the press, not a complete hardware diagnosis.</p>
  <h2>Test individual keyboard keys systematically</h2>
  <p>Work through the part of the keyboard that concerns you rather than pressing random keys. If a letter feels unreliable, press it 10 to 15 times with normal force and watch whether every fresh press updates the test. If a modifier seems wrong, test it separately and then with a letter. For an external keyboard, repeat the same short set after reconnecting the cable, charging it, or moving its wireless receiver. A written result such as “the E key was detected 9 of 10 times on two browsers” is more useful than saying the keyboard feels inconsistent.</p>
  <h2>Why a key may not appear in a browser test</h2>
  <p>Focus is the first thing to check. Click the test control and try again, because key events go to the currently focused element. Some shortcuts are intentionally handled by the operating system, browser, accessibility software, or a vendor utility before a webpage sees them. Media keys and special function layers may also behave differently from a standard letter key. If one key is absent only in one application but appears here, the issue is more likely application configuration than an immediately provable keyboard switch problem.</p>
  <h2>Keyboard layouts, repeats, and modifiers</h2>
  <p>A keyboard layout can change the printed character without changing the physical key you pressed. Auto-repeat can also create repeated keydown events when a key is held, which is not the same as repeatedly pressing and releasing it. For that reason, make individual checks with deliberate releases between presses. Test left and right modifiers separately when relevant: a shortcut may depend on one specific key position. This page does not block normal key behavior broadly, so avoid system shortcuts that could navigate away or change another application.</p>
  <h2>Use focused follow-up tools</h2>
  <p>Individual keys and combinations answer different questions. Use the <a href="/keyboard-ghosting-test">Keyboard Ghosting Test</a> when a key works alone but seems to disappear while W, A, S, D, or Space are held together. Use the <a href="/spacebar-test">Spacebar Test</a> when the long Space key misses presses or repeats unexpectedly. These focused pages keep one intent per test, making a result easier to reproduce and explain.</p>
  <h2>Limits of an online keyboard test</h2>
  <p>An online keyboard test cannot inspect a switch, keycap stabilizer, cable, receiver, battery, firmware, or operating-system driver. It cannot confirm how a particular game captures raw input. It can, however, provide a neutral browser comparison. Try the same keyboard on another computer, or try a known-good keyboard on the same computer, before deciding whether the fault follows the device. Clean or service a keyboard only according to its manufacturer’s guidance, especially if it is wireless or has a built-in battery.</p>
</section>}

{current === 'ghosting' && <section class="seo-content">
  <h2>How to run a keyboard ghosting test</h2>
  <p>This keyboard ghosting test shows the simultaneous key presses that reach a focused browser panel. Click the panel, hold two keys, then add a third key such as Space. A useful first combination is W + A + Space because it resembles movement and jump controls, but the best test is the exact combination that fails in your game or application. The live count shows how many keys are held now, while the peak records the largest combination seen during the current run.</p>
  <h2>What keyboard ghosting means</h2>
  <p>Keyboard ghosting is commonly used to describe a limitation where certain simultaneous key combinations do not register as expected, or appear to produce an unintended key. The result can depend on the keyboard’s matrix design and the exact row-and-column combination, not only the total number of keys. A keyboard may accept three keys in one pattern and reject three keys in another. This page therefore displays the combination the browser received rather than assigning a pass or fail label to every keyboard.</p>
  <h2>Test combinations, not just individual keys</h2>
  <p>A key that works in the <a href="/keyboard-test">Keyboard Test</a> can still be affected when held with other keys. Begin by confirming each key independently, then hold the first two keys for a moment before adding the third. Release all keys and repeat the same sequence three to five times. Note the exact combination, including left versus right modifier keys where relevant. This approach avoids confusing a momentary mistype or an unfocused tab with a combination-specific input limitation.</p>
  <h2>How browser key events affect the result</h2>
  <p>The browser can report only events delivered to the page. The standard behavior and key values for this interaction are documented in the <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/uievents-key/" rel="noopener noreferrer">W3C KeyboardEvent key values specification</a>. Operating-system shortcuts, browser commands, keyboard layouts, accessibility features, and vendor software may handle some keys before the webpage sees them. That is why a missing key here is a useful observation to compare, not proof by itself that a keyboard has a physical defect.</p>
  <h2>Choose useful ghosting test patterns</h2>
  <p>Try movement clusters that matter to you rather than chasing a maximum count. For a PC game, test W + Shift + Space, A + S + D, or the custom bindings you actually use. For music or creative software, try the modifier plus letter sequence that fails in the app. Change only one key between attempts. If W + A + Space works but W + D + Space does not, that comparison is more actionable than a general statement that “three keys do not work.” Write down both the working and failing combinations.</p>
  <h2>What to do when keys are missing</h2>
  <p>First repeat the combination with the test panel focused. Then test another browser, disconnect and reconnect an external keyboard, and check its charge or receiver location if it is wireless. If the same combination fails only in one game, review the game’s bindings and overlays. If it fails across browsers and computers with the same keyboard but not with another keyboard, consult the model’s documentation for rollover or anti-ghosting support. Do not assume every missing combination can be fixed in software.</p>
  <h2>Related keyboard checks</h2>
  <p>For repeatable Space input, use the <a href="/spacebar-test">Spacebar Test</a>; it counts separate Space presses while ignoring normal held-key repeat. For a broader check of buttons and keys, use the standard keyboard tool first. The purpose of this ghosting test is deliberately narrow: it captures simultaneous input so that you can compare one specific combination before changing hardware, remapping controls, or contacting support.</p>
</section>}

{current === 'spacebar' && <section class="seo-content">
  <h2>How to use this spacebar test</h2>
  <p>This spacebar test counts separate presses of the Space key after you focus the large test control. Click the control once, press and release Space, and watch the counter increase. Each new physical-style press updates the time, key code, and status. The test intentionally ignores automatic repeat events generated when you hold the key down. That makes it more useful for checking whether ordinary press-and-release actions reach the browser, rather than measuring how quickly your system repeats a held character.</p>
  <h2>Test a spacebar with a controlled sample</h2>
  <p>Start with 10 slow presses using the center of the bar, leaving a short pause after every release. Then repeat near the left and right ends if your keyboard’s long key feels different in those positions. Record whether the count rises once for every intended press. A simple result such as “two misses in 30 slow presses, including one near the right edge” gives you a useful baseline. Avoid judging the key from one missed press, because focus, an incomplete release, or an interrupted test can produce a misleading result.</p>
  <h2>Why a spacebar can feel inconsistent</h2>
  <p>The spacebar is wider than a letter key and often uses a stabilizer as well as a switch. Dirt, wear, a loose keycap, connection conditions, a low wireless battery, a layout change, or software that captures a shortcut can all create a symptom that feels like a missed press. This page does not inspect any of those physical parts; it shows the key events the browser receives. Compare the result after one change at a time, such as refocusing the page, reconnecting the keyboard, or trying another browser.</p>
  <h2>Understand Space key events</h2>
  <p>Browsers expose key values and codes for keyboard interaction. The <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/uievents-key/" rel="noopener noreferrer">W3C KeyboardEvent key values specification</a> defines the vocabulary used to identify keys, including Space. The displayed code gives a repeatable way to describe what this test received. A result here does not guarantee that every game, text editor, media player, or operating-system shortcut will behave the same way, because those programs can process keys at different levels.</p>
  <h2>Keep focus on the spacebar test</h2>
  <p>Space commonly scrolls a webpage when no suitable control has focus. That is why this page asks you to click the test panel before pressing it. Once focused, the test prevents the normal page scroll for Space and records the press. If the page scrolls instead, click the panel again and retry. Browser or system-level shortcuts can still take priority in some setups. Do not rely on a page result for keys reserved by your operating system or an accessibility tool.</p>
  <h2>Compare the spacebar with other keyboard tests</h2>
  <p>Use the <a href="/keyboard-test">Keyboard Test</a> to compare Space with letters, arrows, modifiers, and other individual keys. If Space works alone but seems to fail while you move with W or A, use the <a href="/keyboard-ghosting-test">Keyboard Ghosting Test</a> to check the exact held combination. The tools have different scopes: this page counts distinct Space presses, while the ghosting page looks at keys held at the same time.</p>
  <h2>When to seek hardware support</h2>
  <p>Try the keyboard on another computer before treating the switch as the cause. For a wired model, use a known-good port directly rather than a hub. For a wireless model, charge it, move the receiver closer, and remove nearby sources of interference where practical. If a repeatable missed-press pattern follows the same keyboard across systems, keep the count and conditions as a short support record. Follow the manufacturer’s instructions before removing a keycap or cleaning around a stabilizer.</p>
</section>}

{current === 'reaction' && <section class="seo-content">
  <h2>How to use this reaction time test</h2>
  <p>This reaction time test measures the interval between the panel turning green and the click that reaches the browser. Click once to arm a round, wait for the random delay, then click only after the color changes. The result is shown in milliseconds: 250 ms means one quarter of a second. Complete several rounds before comparing scores. A single fast round can come from anticipation, while a single slow round can come from a distraction, an awkward mouse position, or an early loss of focus.</p>
  <h2>Run repeatable reaction time rounds</h2>
  <p>Use five to ten rounds in a quiet setting, with the same pointing device and seated position. Look at both your best and your typical result. The best shows the quickest successful response in that sample; the other rounds show how consistent the result was. Avoid clicking before the signal. The page marks an early click as too soon and does not include it as a reaction measurement, because no visible cue had occurred for you to respond to.</p>
  <h2>What the reaction time number includes</h2>
  <p>The number is a browser-side measurement, not a medical assessment. It includes your visual recognition and movement to click, plus a small amount of display, device, operating-system, and browser timing. This page uses a high-resolution browser clock; the <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/hr-time-3/" rel="noopener noreferrer">W3C High Resolution Time specification</a> describes an API designed to provide a stable time origin and sub-millisecond-resolution timestamps. That still does not turn an online result into a measurement of the nervous system alone.</p>
  <h2>Why reaction time results vary</h2>
  <p>Sleep, attention, caffeine, stress, practice, hand position, pointer-device feel, display refresh behavior, and background activity can all affect a round. Anticipating the color change can make a score appear unusually quick, which is why the waiting interval changes randomly. Do not compare a laptop trackpad test with a gaming mouse test as though they were identical conditions. If you want to compare progress, use the same device, browser, time of day, and number of rounds for each session.</p>
  <h2>Interpret a reaction time test responsibly</h2>
  <p>Use this tool for casual practice and personal comparison. It is not designed to diagnose attention, eyesight, neurological conditions, or fitness, and it should not be used to make medical, employment, driving, or safety decisions. A series of results is more informative than a one-off score. If a result seems implausibly fast, repeat the run without trying to predict the cue. If you clicked early, reset mentally and wait for the next randomized signal rather than treating that attempt as an achievement.</p>
  <h2>Separate reaction speed from click reliability</h2>
  <p>If a click does not seem to register reliably, the number from a reaction round may be hard to interpret. Use the <a href="/double-click-test">Double Click Test</a> to look for unusually close repeated clicks during slow presses, or use the <a href="/mouse-test">Mouse Test</a> to check basic button, wheel, and movement events. If your goal is fast clicking rather than visual response, use the <a href="/cps-test">CPS Test</a>; it has a fixed five-second scoring window and answers a different question.</p>
  <h2>Make the test fair across attempts</h2>
  <p>Keep the browser tab visible, avoid changing windows while a round is armed, and use the same click target each time. Do not refresh in the middle of a comparison, because a new run begins with no prior context. Record the number of completed rounds alongside your best and approximate average. These simple controls make a casual reaction time test more credible and make it easier to tell whether a change in score reflects practice, a different setup, or ordinary variation.</p>
</section>}
